  "worked_solution": "If $A={U}_{r}{\u03a3}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{\u03a3}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\u03a3}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${\u03a3}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{\u03a3}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{\u03a3}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{250}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{250}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{250}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
